# WORKFLOW — Write Map, Tempo, Swarm, Probing & Lore

File: openOODA/WORKFLOW.oot
Logline: Tells agents what to change and where, then how to run the turn.
Setup: Read section 0 before any edit. Write ASD-STE100 (RULES 1.10). Paths are from this folder (openOODA/).
Beats:
  0. Product write map (what and where)
  1. OODA loop tempo
  2. Swarm protocols
  3. Hostile probing
  4. Tokenomics
  5. Bug lore

## 0. Product Write Map — What Changes Where

Three public places. That is the whole public system.
  Door     ../openOODA.github.io/index.html     https://openooda.org/
  Guide    ../openOODA.github.io/guide/*.oot    https://openooda.org/guide/
  Store    ../registry/                         https://registry.openooda.org/
Do not add research to nav. Files under ../openOODA.github.io/research/ stay so old URLs do not 404.

### 0.1 If you ship this, write here

  Package manager / ooda add / ingest / launch
    ../ooda/cli/cmd_pm*.oo  ../ooda/cli/pm_*.oo  ../ooda/cli/main.oo
    Proof: ../ooda/cli/*_proof.oo and ../qa/pm_*_spine.oo
    ../ooda/pm/ is a stub. Do not grow it.
    Do not edit ../ooda/oodac/, ../ooda/runtime/, or ../ooda/std/ unless the owner named that lane.

  Compiler / language
    ../ooda/oodac/*.oo and named ../ooda/scripts/*_smoke.oo
    Official rebuild: ../ooda/bootstrap/oodac_pure_build
    Never copy ./bin/ooda onto ./oodac/oodac.

  Runtime C
    ../ooda/runtime/ — RT role only.
  Standard library
    ../ooda/std/  Ships with the compiler. Prove emit+gcc+run before you call a folder product.

  Safety that is true today
    ../openOODA.github.io/guide/Safety.oot
    New guide page: add the .oot and add its name to guide/index.html PAGES.

  How-to, options, limits
    ../openOODA.github.io/guide/{Home,Use,Options,Limits}.oot

  Design / moonshot / not shipped
    ../openOODA.github.io/research/*.oot stay on disk. Do not link them from the door or the guide.
    Do not add a research nav item. Do not paste moonshots into the guide as if they were product.

  Public install
    From a checkout:
      OODA_FS_WRITEDIR=$HOME OODA_CHECKOUT=$PWD ooda install
    Payload is .oo (openOODA.github.io/install). Never | sh.
    Real installer: ../ooda/install/install.oo
    First machine still needs the tree, gcc, and the seed rebuild.

  Public package
    ../registry/<name>/manifest and payload .oo
    Rewrite registry/index (pipe rows). Copy those bytes to catalog.
    Sign: minisign -S -s ~/.ooda-keys/registry.secret.key -m index -x index.minisig
    Update human index.html. CLI verifies catalog with ../ooda/cli/registry.pub
    Never commit the secret. Browsers hit /. CLI GETs /catalog.

  Process, pin, proved inventory
    The nine boards in this folder. SHIPPED only after proof.

  Hostile product proofs
    ../qa/pm_*_spine.oo or ../ooda/scripts/*_smoke.oo

### 0.2 Do not write

  ../wiki/ and ../docs/    local clones removed. GitHub wiki and docs repos archived.
  leftover GitHub repos named research or install
                           those steal https://openooda.org/research and /install.
  Guide lives at /guide/. /research/ is an archive URL, not a nav item.
  ~/.ooda-keys/            secrets stay off git.

### 0.3 Site rules and publish

  One-bit Macintosh: white or black, monospace, title-bar wordmark.
  Theme toggle top-right. GitHub link bottom-right. Cookie ooda-theme on .openooda.org.
  Public links stay on openooda.org. Link github.com only to visit GitHub.
  Site and registry: commit and push. GitHub Pages serves them.
  Product: commit in ../ooda/. Do not push secrets.

## 1. OODA Loop Tempo & Rotation Lifecycle

The OODA rotation guarantees continuous progress, verifiable quality, and clean git history on every turn.

1. Observe
  - Read section 0 so the edit lands in the right tree.
  - Read active handoffs and release pin in `ROADMAP.oot`.
  - Skim Section 5 (Bug Lore) below to avoid repeating past failure modes.
  - Inspect repository health: file line counts (<= 256 lines), cruft, failed tests.
  - Identify I/O, subprocess execution, FFI, or capability attack surfaces.
  - Review SWARM requirements: decide whether this turn is solo or fan-out.
  - Define the single best next product step before taking action.

2. Decide (At Most 5 Items)
  Prioritize tasks in strict order:
    1. Broken tests and failed product rail checks.
    2. Honesty and security vulnerabilities that permit invalid execution.
    3. Next scheduled backlog item directly serving DESIGN.
    4. DESIGN architectural gap (document in backlog first, then build).
    5. Code hygiene and refactoring (if blocking or cheap).
  Decide Swarm Topology:
    - Solo: single agent owning a focused task; document reason ("solo under SWARM: <why>").
    - Fan-out: multiple subagents with strict file isolation or read-only roles.
    - Anti-Pattern: Never assign multiple agents to debug the same stuck issue.

3. Act
  - Write comments, boards, and user text in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English.
  - Implement changes strictly on the real product path.
  - Execute under the SWARM plan determined during Decide.
  - Maintain the 256-line ceiling per file; split modules at clean seams.
  - Deliver executable tests with behavioral assertions alongside code changes.
  - Zero secrets in codebase, configs, or logs.

4. Lock
  - Run RULES 1.7 verification: line counts, dead code, secrets, temporary artifacts.
  - Execute project rails and pure `.oo` test suites.
  - Independently verify all assertions; never mark a task done without direct proof.
  - Ensure no orphaned background subagents or dangling worker processes remain.
  - If hygiene fails, do not proceed to Ship: repair violations immediately.

5. Ship
  - Create atomic commits with descriptive messages.
  - Write the handoff in ASD-STE100: what changed, why it matters, what is not done.
  - Record SWARM execution topology: solo rationale or fan-out role breakdown.

Session Adapter Template:
  ROOT: /path/to/project
  Product binaries / build command: ./bin/ooda or oodac_pure_build
  Backlog / Handoff: ROADMAP.oot
  Rails: ./bin/ooda check_all / scripts/dinner_lint.oo
  SWARM plan: solo (<reason>) OR fan-out (<agent>: <owned files>)

## 2. Multi-Agent Swarm Protocols & Phalanx Architecture

Multi-agent coordination maximizes throughput while strictly conserving token context.

Core Swarm Protocols:
  1. Right-Size the Model:
     - Light/Flash models: hygiene, line-locks, linting, formatting, single command runs.
     - Heavy/Pro models: compiler architecture, AST typechecking, complex multi-file bugs.
  2. Small Ships:
     - Assign each agent a single, well-scoped objective (typically one file).
     - Terminate execution upon goal completion or first irrecoverable failure.
  3. Stuck Protocols:
     - If blocked, diagnose directly with `grep`, `tail`, and isolated CLI commands.
     - Terminate rogue or hanging processes promptly.
  4. Strict File Isolation:
     - Concurrent writers must own disjoint sets of files.
     - If overlapping edits are needed, serialize execution or branch separate worktrees.
  5. Adversarial Red-Teaming:
     - QA subagents actively construct counter-examples to falsify happy-path claims.
     - QA identifies and documents the vulnerability; a builder subagent refactors the code.

The 12-Agent Phalanx Architecture (Major Releases & Refactors):
  - Engineering:
      DE1, DE2 : Implement core product features and compiler passes.
      RT       : Maintains C runtime (`chs_rt_*.c`) and emit boundaries.
  - Adversarial:
      PV1, PV2 : Red-team builder implementations with boundary edge cases.
      SEC      : Audits capability arg-flow, shell escapes, and path traversal.
      MEM      : Inspects allocation limits, heap quotas, and ARC leaks.
  - Integration:
      INT      : Validates end-to-end builds, traces, and link integrity.
      HARN     : Develops test fixtures and smoke harnesses.
      HYG      : Enforces 256-line limits, header compliance, and cruft cleanup.
  - Command:
      DOCS     : Synchronizes process boards (`.oot`) with actual codebase status.
      COORD    : Assigns file ownership, manages sprint state, and rolls up reports.

## 3. Hostile Probing & Adversarial Verification

Core Law: Smoke tests are suspects, not proof. Hostile probing actively attacks the assumptions behind code, documentation, and green rails.

Verification Roles:
  - Orchestrator: Schedules test suites, coordinates runs, and compiles audit reports.
  - Worker: Injects faults, runs mutation tests, and implements fixes on product binaries.
  - Independent Verifier: Re-executes claims in fresh environments; rejects any test that would pass if the defect remained.

Double-Run Law:
  Every CRIT severity finding requires two independent, reproducible runs in clean shells.

Probing Vectors:
  1. Boundary:
     - Missing capability token fails closed.
     - Forged, zero, or un-granted tokens are rejected at runtime.
     - Disk exhaustion, permission drops, and short writes trigger explicit errors.
  2. Robustness:
     - Resource exhaustion: low file descriptors, tight heap limits without crashing.
     - Read-only directories: non-zero exit on write attempts.
     - Concurrent garbage input: no deadlocks or state corruption.
     - Hang budget: 5 to 10 seconds maximum per input; any hang is treated as a hard failure.
  3. Lifecycle:
     - All opened file and socket descriptors closed across error and success paths.
     - Child processes cleanly reaped without zombie retention.
     - Temporary files deleted in `finally` / teardown routines.
  4. Anti-Synthetic Verification:
     - Test the exact binaries distributed to end users.
     - Validate unmasked return codes directly (no masking behind shell pipes).
     - Test one layer below the claim to expose shallow mocking.

Finding Severity Hierarchy:
  - CRIT: Security bypass, capability forgery, or unauthorized success on product path.
  - HIGH: Fail-open behavior, execution hang, silent data corruption, or dropped errors.
  - MED:  Handle/memory leaks, incomplete capability seals, or hygiene debt.
  - LOW:  Documentation drift, comment mismatch, or minor metric discrepancies.

## 4. Token Economy & Tokenomics Reference

Context is finite. Verbose prose steals the reasoning budget.
Canonical files mark `canonical: yes` and may point at a short-form sibling.
Short-form marks `canonical: no`. Use canonical for review and design.
Use short-form for large multi-file LLM migrations.
Short-form may minify locals and drop extra comments. Keep public signatures.

## 5. Hard-Won System Lore & Bug Lessons

1. AST Injection:
   C macro injection inside comma expressions cannot parse un-braced constructs. Always inject `((void)0)` instead of `do {} while(0)`. (Learned 2026-08-11)
2. Decoy Memory Offsets:
   The compiler shuffles `__oo_meta_decoy_*` struct layouts at runtime. Tests must never assume fixed static struct offsets. (Learned 2026-08-12)
3. HITL & Process Environment Policy:
   Human-in-the-loop tests and configuration lookups must invoke `oo_process_policy_getenv` to respect security policy rather than raw host `getenv`. (Learned 2026-08-11)
4. Capability Gating:
   All newly introduced standard library, cryptographic, or network primitives must fail closed with `process_exit(1)` until formal audit verification lands. (Learned 2026-08-12)
5. AST Imports:
   Import tokens must be represented as distinct `IMPORT` AST nodes to ensure file boundaries remain concrete and `item_idx` increments accurately. (Learned 2026-08-12)
6. Scripting vs Compiler Conventions:
   All smoke tests and utility scripts are pure `.oo` files located under `ooda/scripts/*_smoke.oo`. Never create `.oos`, `.sh`, or `.py` files. (Learned 2026-08-13)
7. FFI Handle Serialization:
   Host dynamic library operations (`oo_dlopen`) require `&UnsafeFFICap` and must lock the FFI handle mutex to prevent nested invocation races. (Learned 2026-08-14)
8. Product CLI string traps:
   Do not write `str_index_of(...) == 0` or `!= 0`. Do not write `String != String`.
   Do not mutate a slurp String named `lock`, `lk`, or `src`. `"\r"` is not CR.
   Writes go through `write_file` and `oo_fs_mkdir` under `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.
9. Never copy CLI onto compiler. Identity is `grep -a` only. No Cargo. No ELF transmutation.

